    23	Logs and privacy-safe reports are retained under .temp/TASK-260715-39xz9g/.     1	# SSH transport specification
     2	
     3	## Transport interface
     4	
     5	The packet tunnel extension embeds a native SSH client behind this conceptual
     6	interface:
     7	
     8	```swift
     9	protocol SSHTransport {
    10	    func connect(profile: SSHProfile) async throws -> SSHSession
    11	    func openDirectTCPIP(
    12	        destination: Endpoint,
    13	        originator: Endpoint,
    14	        policy: ChannelPolicy
    15	    ) async throws -> SSHByteChannel
    16	    func openExecChannel(command: String) async throws -> SSHExecChannel
    17	    func requestRekey() async throws
    18	    func metrics() async -> SSHTransportMetrics
    19	    func close() async
    20	}
    21	```
    22	
    23	The concrete API may differ, but the abstraction MUST provide host-key evidence,
    24	bounded writes/backpressure, per-channel window policy, server-initiated rekey,
    25	client-initiated rekey by byte/time threshold, keepalive, cancellation, and
    26	connection metrics. SFTP is intentionally excluded.
    27	
    28	## Engine decision (ADR-014, ADR-023)
    29	
    30	Vanilla SwiftNIO SSH is not an acceptable production engine at the inspected
    31	upstream commit because child channel receive windows are hard-coded to 16 MiB
    32	and client-initiated rekey is internal/test-only. Three rounds of pinned-source
    33	analysis then showed that the original full-fidelity contract also exceeded the
    34	public surface of *every* candidate, including `libssh2`.
    35	
    36	**libssh2 is the selected primary engine** (ADR-014). The minimal `ReluxNIOSSH`
    37	fork is retained as recorded comparative evidence and receives no further fork
    38	work unless new evidence invalidates libssh2.
    39	
    40	The contract is therefore split into two tiers. Both are binding; they differ
    41	only in *when* the evidence is owed. The exhaustive normative classification,
    42	neutral seam mapping, pinned blocker evidence, and consumer mapping live in
    43	[`ssh-transport-conformance-contract.md`](ssh-transport-conformance-contract.md).
    44	
    45	### Tier 1 — M0-viability-mandatory
    46	
    47	The selected engine MUST pass every row before the working macOS client path
    48	proceeds. A red row routes to the alternate engine or back to analysis; it is
    49	never a reason to weaken a row.
    50	
    51	| Gate | Required evidence |
    52	| --- | --- |
    53	| Apple targets | Static or source integration works in the macOS harness and the macOS packet-tunnel extension (iOS deferred, ADR-024) |
    54	| Host verification | Raw host key/fingerprint is available **before** authentication acceptance |
    55	| Authentication | Ed25519 and at least one broadly deployed fallback key type work against target servers |
    56	| Direct TCP | Concurrent `direct-tcpip` channels open, backpressure, and close independently at the counts the macOS harness can drive |
    57	| Exec | Bidirectional stdio exec channel supports relay bootstrap, exec/stdin upload, and long-lived framing |
    58	| Client rekey | Client-initiated rekey by byte and time threshold succeeds under active traffic |
    59	| Server rekey handling | Inbound server KEX preserves active channel traffic and bounded buffers; deep lifecycle/generation telemetry is Tier 2 |
    60	| Algorithms | The real `relux` server and a documented compatibility matrix pass |
    61	| Bounded memory | Buffers, queues, and advertised credit stay bounded and inside the recorded harness budget |
    62	| Lifecycle | Cancellation, network loss, and reconnect do not leak channels, tasks, sockets, or descriptors |
    63	| Errors | Failures map to privacy-safe categories with no host, credential, or path leakage |
    64	| Keychain-only secrets | The seam carries opaque credential references and external signing only; private keys/passphrases stay in the approved Keychain-backed provider |
    65	| Keepalive | Configured bounded SSH keepalive transmission and available failure behavior remain operational |
    66	| Available observability | Cheaply available typed metrics/events remain privacy-safe; unavailable deep fields use explicit deferred states |
    67	
    68	### Tier 2 — M3 evidence-gated (deferred, not waived)
    69	
    70	M0 adapters MUST surface each of these as an explicit not-reported or
    71	unsupported state. Fabricating a value, or silently substituting a weaker
    72	behavior, fails the adapter.
    73	
    74	| Deferred semantic | Why it is deferred | Owner |
    75	| --- | --- | --- |
    76	| Consumer-driven receive-window credit with an immutable per-channel cap | `libssh2_channel_read` auto-adjusts credit on read entry, before consumer delivery; NIOSSH has the equivalent frame-delivery problem | `TASK-260728-3cveay` |
    77	| RFC 4254 channel-open rejection reason taxonomy | libssh2 collapses the RFC reason codes | `TASK-260728-3cveay` |
    78	| Exact exec-exit metadata: `status(0)` vs `notReported`, and `coreDumped` | The public surface cannot distinguish absent metadata from status 0 | `TASK-260728-3cveay` |
    79	| Deep rekey/keepalive observability: server-initiated KEX lifecycle and generation, reply-correlated keepalive RTT/timeout/miss | No server-KEX lifecycle or reply-correlated global-request result is exposed | `TASK-260728-3cveay` |
    80	
    81	Multi-gigabyte rekey soak, staged hundreds-of-channels scale, and extension
    82	memory-budget numbers are M3 physical evidence
    83	(`TASK-260715-2xx2tk`, `TASK-260715-1k3wsk`), not M0 selection inputs.
    84	
    85	## Lane pool
    86	
    87	One TCP connection creates cross-flow head-of-line blocking. The transport uses
    88	two to four SSH connections to the same host:
    89	
    90	- **Lane A — control:** relay exec, health, and latency-sensitive DNS/control;
    91	- **Lanes B and C — general:** ordinary TCP flows;
    92	- **Lane D — bulk:** optional/reserved capacity for new likely bulk flows.
    93	
    94	All lanes use the same verified host identity and profile credentials. Opening
    95	optional lanes is constrained by memory pressure and server limits.
    96	
    97	A flow is pinned to one lane until close. Live flows are never migrated because
    98	that would reorder the byte stream. New flows use congestion-aware assignment
    99	based on writable window, queued bytes, RTT/health, open-channel count, port,
   100	and best-effort destination metadata. Lane D cannot retroactively adopt a flow

